course: everyday
lesson: 353
title: When you want what others have
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At a party in Toronto, Leo slipped a second goody bag into his coat while nobody was looking. Nobody caught him — but at the door the last little girl got nothing, and her mum had to say «sorry, sweetheart, they've run out». On the way home the bag felt heavy in his pocket. He did not eat any of it. That evening he told Mum, and the next day they took the bag back and he said sorry to the party mum, who hugged him and said «thank you for telling me».
explain: Chloe's sister came home with a gold certificate for spelling. Chloe, who had got a sticker that week, felt hot and jealous and tore the certificate in half. Her sister cried and showed Mum. Chloe lost her pocket money for two weeks and stamped upstairs shouting «you always love her more». Later, when she had calmed down, she said sorry and made her sister a new certificate with gold pen and glitter.
explain: Envy whispers that someone else's good thing is taken from you. It is not: your sister's good mark does not lower yours, and your cousin's holiday does not shorten your summer — call it boring, and you only miss his happy stories. Feeling envy is normal; acting on it to hurt someone is the wrong part.
explain: Greed for more and more things hides what you already have. Saying thank you, sharing and saving up turn wanting into something good.
fact: Feeling jealous is a normal feeling; what matters is what you do with it.
fact: Someone else's success does not take anything away from you.
fact: Saying thank you for a present tells the giver their care was noticed, which often means more to them than the present.
fact: Making someone hand something over because they are scared of you is bullying, not a fair swap.
fact: Fair does not always mean the same: an older child may get more pocket money for doing more jobs, and a younger one can ask for a job to earn a little more.
fact: Sharing food with a friend who has none is one of the simplest kindnesses.
fact: Wanting something because «everyone has one» is called peer pressure.
fact: Even when nobody sees a wrong, the person who did it often feels guilty, and the guilt lasts until it is put right.
fact: Losing pocket money for damaging something is a consequence, not a sign that you are loved less.
fact: Owning up when nobody caught you is usually met with more trust, not less.
exercise: Why did Leo take a second goody bag? | He wanted more than his share | The party mum told him to take it | He was taking it for his sister | He thought it was his own goody bag
exercise: Nobody caught Leo. Who still paid for what he did? | The last little girl, who got no bag | Only Leo's mum, who paid for the bags | Nobody at all | The shop that sold the bags
exercise: How did Leo feel on the way home, even though he was not caught? | Guilty, as if the bag were heavy | Proud of being so clever and quick | Happy and hungry | He felt nothing at all
exercise: What put Leo's wrong right? | Telling Mum, giving the bag back and saying sorry | Eating it all quickly, so that nobody would ever know | Giving it to his brother | Keeping it until next year
exercise: Chloe tore her sister's certificate. Which feeling drove her? | Envy and anger | Tiredness | Kindness | Boredom
exercise: Does her sister's gold certificate make Chloe's sticker worth less? | No, her sister's success takes nothing from Chloe | Yes, there is only so much praise in one family to share | Yes, stickers lose value next to gold | Only if they are in the same class
exercise: Chloe lost two weeks of pocket money and shouted «you love her more». What is closer to the truth? | The pocket money is a consequence of tearing it, not less love | Mum really does love her sister more, and this is how she shows it | Mum wanted to keep the money | It was a random punishment
exercise: Jayden kept asking Gran for more presents and never said thank you. What did Gran feel? | That he did not care about her | That he loved her more each time | That he was always very polite to her | Nothing, grans never notice such things
exercise: What put things right with Gran? | Writing her a real thank-you letter | Asking for a bigger present | Sending her a list for next year | Saying thank you only for the best ones
exercise: Harry made a younger boy give him his football because the boy was scared. Why is that wrong? | Making someone give you something by fear is bullying | It is fine if the ball is better used by someone who plays more | The boy could buy another one | It is a normal swap
exercise: Ruby counted her brother's pocket money and said it was not fair he got more. Mum explained he is older and does more jobs. Which is true? | Fair does not always mean the same | Fair always means exactly the same amount | Older children should always get less | Pocket money should always be kept secret
exercise: What wise step did Ruby take after that? | She asked for a job so she could earn more | She told Gran that Mum was being unfair | She hid her brother's money | She refused to do any jobs
exercise: Max would not share his chips with a friend who forgot his lunch. His friend was hungry all afternoon. What put it right? | Saying sorry and sharing his lunch the next day | Telling his friend to remember his own lunch next time | Saying the chips were too few | Buying chips only for himself
exercise: Aisha kept asking for the newest phone «because everyone has one». What is this called? | Peer pressure | Saving up money | Having good manners | Being grateful
exercise: Leo's cousin says «you should've just kept it, nobody saw». What is the wise answer? | Someone still went without, and I'd feel bad keeping it | Yes, if nobody saw it's fine, there were loads of bags left | Yes, goody bags don't count | Next time take three
exercise: Ella says her cousin's holiday sounds «boring» because she is jealous. What does she miss? | Hearing her cousin's happy stories | Nothing, holidays are boring | The chance to go on the holiday | Her own holiday next year
test: «literally everyone in my class has the new phone except me, mum's basically punishing me». What's really going on? | It's peer pressure; saying no is not a punishment | Every child in her class truly has one, so Mum is wrong | Mum is punishing her for nothing | Phones are a basic need at nine
test: big sis got student of the month and little bro goes «whatever, it's a rubbish award». What's the kind thing? | Say well done to her | Agree that it's rubbish | Tell her to give it back | Say nothing to her all week
test: «he gave me his ball, i didn't even hit him, just told him to». Is that a fair way to get it? | No, scaring someone into giving is bullying | Yes, if you don't hit him, it's just talking | Yes, if the ball is old | Only if he's younger
test: Gran on the phone: «Did the jumper fit, love? You never said.» What's the best thing to do now? | Thank her properly and tell her about it | Ask for a different present next time | Say you never got it | Hand the phone to Mum
test: took 2 goody bags last time and nobody noticed, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, someone went without even if nobody noticed | Yes, there are always spares at the end of a party | Yes, if nobody noticed | Only at big parties
test: «why does HE get £10 and i get £5, it's NOT fair». He's 13 and mows the lawn. What's true? | Fair doesn't always mean the same; he's older and does more | It's always unfair to get different amounts, whatever the reason | He should give you half | You should get more for being younger
test: mate forgot his lunch, you've got loads of chips. other mate says «don't, he'll just do it again». Best move? | Share some today; he's hungry now | Eat them all in front of him | Sell him some at double price | Throw the rest away
test: Mum's text: «Your sister's certificate is in TWO PIECES. I want to know who did this.» You did. Best reply? | «It was me. I'm sorry, I'll make her a new one» | «Ask her, she's careless with her things» | «Probably the cat» | Don't answer
test: «cousin's been to Disneyland AGAIN, i'm sick of hearing about it». What's the wiser feeling to aim for? | Be glad for him and ask about the best part | Make up a better holiday to tell him about at once | Tell him it sounds boring | Stop talking to him
test: You told Mum the truth about the goody bag. She says «I'm proud of you». Why might she trust you more now? | Owning up when nobody caught you shows honesty | Because goody bags are cheap and easy to replace | She didn't care about the bag | Mum always trusts children
test: «i'll say thank you to Nan only if the present's good». What's wrong with that? | Thanks is for her care, not the price | Nothing, only good presents count | Nan won't notice either way | Thank-yous are only for birthdays
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between another person's achievement and one's own? | Another person's achievement does not diminish one's own | One person's success necessarily reduces another's | Achievements are rationed within a family | Envy increases one's own achievement
exam: It is given that a child obtained an article from another child by intimidation, without the use of force. Identify the correct characterisation. | It constitutes bullying and the article must be returned | It is acceptable in the absence of force or of any physical threat | It constitutes a lawful exchange | It is permissible if the article is of low value
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the unequal distribution of pocket money between siblings of differing age and duties. | Fairness does not invariably require equal amounts | Fairness invariably requires identical amounts | The younger sibling is entitled to the larger sum | Pocket money ought to be abolished
exam: State the principal reason for which an undetected wrong may nonetheless require amends. | Another person has suffered a loss regardless of detection | Amends are required only following a complaint by the injured party | Detection is the sole source of obligation | Undetected wrongs cease to exist
